WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:01.269
Addiction.

00:00:01.269 --> 00:00:06.830
It takes many forms and wears many faces,
like a demon walking among us.

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:12.320
It takes over your life - crawling in and
changing you from the inside out.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.390
Breaking an addiction is hard, but oftentimes
not giving it up is much harder.

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:23.870
That was a difficult lesson to learn for three
people: Joey Walker, Dolly McGregor, and Paul

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:24.870
Ables.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:30.340
These three had never met one another, and
now, they never will, but each of them had

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.700
two things in common.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.680
First was that they each had an addiction
they felt like they couldn’t shake, and

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:41.570
the second was that they each had a run-in
with SCP-666.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:44.720
Joey Walker was at the end of his rope when
he encountered it.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:50.239
Everyone has bad days, but for Joey, every
day for the last fifteen years has been a

00:00:50.239 --> 00:00:51.330
bad day.

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:56.650
He was pushing 40 with nothing to show for
it, except credit card debt, a scraggly beard,

00:00:56.650 --> 00:00:58.420
and a beat-up old Mustang.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.809
But booze had always been there for him.

00:01:00.809 --> 00:01:06.010
No matter how bad things got, he could always
count on a glass full of comfort at the end

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:07.100
of the day.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:12.530
But to paraphrase a quote by Stephen King,
“Sometimes, when a man takes a drink, the

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:14.109
drink takes the man.”

00:01:14.109 --> 00:01:17.400
And Joey Walker had been long since taken.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:22.740
He was a transient worker, doing jobs wherever
jobs needed doing, but he never expected that

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:26.670
he would end up all the way to Tibet working
construction.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:30.810
Not that he was complaining - Sure, it was
cold, but there was plenty of Tibetan Chang

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.030
to enjoy.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:37.909
It was a drink so good that legends even say
that Yetis would raid villages in the mountains

00:01:37.909 --> 00:01:39.969
to get a little taste.

00:01:39.969 --> 00:01:44.469
Joey had taken a liking to the Chang, and
it was what he had been drinking when he crashed

00:01:44.469 --> 00:01:48.359
his car up in the mountains, leaving him stranded
in the snow.

00:01:48.359 --> 00:01:53.750
Joey had survived the crash itself, but he
knew he wouldn’t last long in the cold.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:54.750
He needed shelter.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:58.759
That’s why he felt like he could hardly
believe his luck when he first saw the Yurt

00:01:58.759 --> 00:02:02.969
- a kind of traditional, Tibetan tent made
from animal skins.

00:02:02.969 --> 00:02:07.739
Joey had never seen anything quite like it,
but any refuge from the storm would do.

00:02:07.739 --> 00:02:11.160
He pushed forward through the snow and climbed
inside.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.110
But the inside of the Yurt was nothing like
the outside.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:19.650
In fact, it seemed like an exact replica of
his favorite sports bar back in Atlantic City.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:24.950
It was warm, there was all the old memorabilia
on the walls, and every screen was showing

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:29.530
the Saints wiping the floor with the Colts
in the 2010 Superbowl.

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:30.970
Joey didn’t even question it.

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:33.230
He just smiled, and approached the bar.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:38.250
He even recognized the bartender - Malcolm,
a good old friend of his.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:43.110
Malcolm smiled back at him, but there was
something oddly menacing in his grin.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:44.430
“Welcome back,” he said.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:47.570
“I thought you’d hauled your sorry keister
off to Tibet.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:51.300
Guess you gave up on that too, just like everything
else.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.400
Well, except…”

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.410
Malcolm pushed a glass of beer towards him
across the bar, and glared.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:02.340
Joey didn’t understand why Malcolm was being
so aggressive, but he didn’t question that,

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:03.340
either.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:07.320
He grabbed the glass and drank up, guzzling
it down before slamming the empty glass down

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.340
on the bar.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:12.430
Malcolm kept the drinks coming, round after
round after round, and Joey kept knocking

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:13.650
them back.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.250
He didn’t notice when the TV screens turned
to static.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:20.960
He didn’t notice when the other patrons
at the bar began to jitter and twitch, turning,

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.040
little by little, into something less than
human.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:29.010
He didn’t even notice when Malcolm’s face
started melting off his skull, as long as

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.680
he kept pouring.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:35.630
Before he could even really understand what
was happening to him, Joey Walker was dead.

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:38.040
His liver and kidneys had given out on him.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.700
His corpse would later be found, frozen on
the Tibetan mountainside, just thirty feet

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:44.730
away from his crashed car.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:48.290
The coroner’s report would list his cause
of death as, “Accidental.”

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:49.940
But this was no accident.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:57.080
He’d fallen victim to SCP-666, an anomalous
Yurt known to some as the Spirit House.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:03.490
The being he’d called Malcolm is known to
the Foundation as SCP-666-1, and Malcolm had

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:05.420
failed its challenge.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:10.840
Sadly for our inebriated friend, the penalty
for failure is death.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:16.889
Next on our list of human tragedies comes
Dolly McGregor, a 62-year-old woman from Anaheim,

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:17.889
California.

00:04:17.889 --> 00:04:23.500
Dolly was never averse to the drink, but she
knew how to enjoy her Mojitos in moderation.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:28.530
You rarely get to 62 if you don’t, and Dolly
could tell people with pride that she had

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:33.759
two children, and six grandchildren, whom
she didn’t see nearly often enough for her

00:04:33.759 --> 00:04:34.759
liking.

00:04:34.759 --> 00:04:41.360
She also had over $100,000 in gambling debt
owed to a number of Casinos on the Vegas Strip.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:46.180
Ever since her husband, Albert, had passed
away several years prior, shooting craps,

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:50.490
playing blackjack, and whiling away the hours
in front of the slot machines had been a welcome

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.550
change from loneliness.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:58.610
But in Vegas, fortunes can change in an instant
- Dolly had gone from being on a hot winning

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:00.680
streak to losing everything.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:05.050
The casinos extended her lines of credit until
she was gambling with money she’d never

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:08.470
be able to pay back, but that didn’t stop
her.

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:13.110
She’d been selling off everything, even
remortgaging the house that Albert had built

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:17.180
with his own two hands to try and keep up
with her ballooning debts.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:21.180
Still, it wasn’t enough, but she couldn’t
stop, either.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:25.169
It wasn’t even that she felt good about
it anymore, it was just that she only ever

00:05:25.169 --> 00:05:30.509
felt normal among the flashing lights of slot
machines and the clatter of dice.

00:05:30.509 --> 00:05:34.449
She needed to get away; as far away as possible.

00:05:34.449 --> 00:05:38.509
She’d heard about people reaching spiritual
enlightenment in Tibet.

00:05:38.509 --> 00:05:44.240
They’d discovered God, or Gods, or sometimes
just a sense of inner peace.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.259
Whatever it was, she needed to find it and
to get this demon off her back.

00:05:48.259 --> 00:05:52.599
So she took the last of her savings and flew
off to Tibet to clear her head.

00:05:52.599 --> 00:05:56.559
She didn’t know if there were any casinos
in Lhasa, but if there were, her money would

00:05:56.559 --> 00:05:58.250
surely be no good there.

00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:01.050
It was exactly what she needed.

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:04.099
But Tibet wasn’t the promised land she thought
it would be.

00:06:04.099 --> 00:06:09.560
Wherever Dolly went, there she was, with the
same vices and demons chasing her.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:14.189
The first night she spent in Lhasa, she used
the hotel wifi to download an online poker

00:06:14.189 --> 00:06:15.610
app on her phone.

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:20.150
This culminated in another couple thousand
dollars lost, and a panic attack that caused

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:25.180
Dolly to throw her phone into her toilet before
wandering off into the wilderness.

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:27.319
She just needed to get away.

00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:32.300
But time and space got away from her, and
eventually, she was knee deep in the snow.

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:33.770
Where had she come from?

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:34.939
Where could she go?

00:06:34.939 --> 00:06:39.551
It quickly dawned on her that, if she didn’t
find some kind of shelter soon, she’d be

00:06:39.551 --> 00:06:40.659
done for.

00:06:40.659 --> 00:06:42.900
But all she could do was keep walking…

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:46.879
That’s when she happened upon SCP-666.

00:06:46.879 --> 00:06:50.350
The Yurt was like an oasis on the barren mountainside.

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:54.539
She didn’t expect comfort inside, but it
could at least provide her some shelter until

00:06:54.539 --> 00:06:57.419
the staff back at the hotel noticed she was
gone.

00:06:57.419 --> 00:07:00.199
Then they’d come and save her, right?

00:07:00.199 --> 00:07:01.199
Right?

00:07:01.199 --> 00:07:06.349
But when she passed through the yak-leather
flap into the Yurt, Dolly wasn’t in an ancient

00:07:06.349 --> 00:07:10.979
tent in the Tibetan wilderness, she was on
a casino floor.

00:07:10.979 --> 00:07:16.259
There was a big, beautiful roulette table
before her, attended to by a grinning, well-dressed

00:07:16.259 --> 00:07:17.259
croupier.

00:07:17.259 --> 00:07:22.009
They were flanked by an army of slot machines,
and all the pleasing, familiar sounds of cheerful

00:07:22.009 --> 00:07:24.129
gambling rang out through the air.

00:07:24.129 --> 00:07:28.039
She approached the table, and the croupier
shook his head in what seemed like disappointment.

00:07:28.039 --> 00:07:31.919
“Back again, eh, Dolly?” he said with
a sigh.

00:07:31.919 --> 00:07:34.499
“Of course, I should have expected it.

00:07:34.499 --> 00:07:38.419
After all, what else do you have in that empty
little life of yours, hmm?

00:07:38.419 --> 00:07:41.309
Without all this, you’d be nothing, right?

00:07:41.309 --> 00:07:42.479
Nothing at all.

00:07:42.479 --> 00:07:46.479
Since Albert died and the kids want nothing
to do with you, we’re all you’ve got.

00:07:46.479 --> 00:07:49.539
So, Dolly, what’ll it be - Red or black?

00:07:49.539 --> 00:07:51.529
Are you feeling lucky?”

00:07:51.529 --> 00:07:55.119
His words wounded Dolly, but that wouldn’t
stop her.

00:07:55.119 --> 00:07:58.210
She grabbed her chips, and put them all on
red.

00:07:58.210 --> 00:08:00.249
One last spin couldn’t hurt.

00:08:00.249 --> 00:08:02.419
What did she have to lose?

00:08:02.419 --> 00:08:05.520
She blinked, and she wasn’t sitting at the
table anymore.

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:07.889
She was upright, but she wasn’t standing.

00:08:07.889 --> 00:08:14.750
Her wrists and ankles were bound to something
- to a wheel, a giant roulette wheel, like

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:18.030
it was some medieval torture device.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.259
The croupier was standing across from her.

00:08:20.259 --> 00:08:23.259
Well, something that used to be a croupier.

00:08:23.259 --> 00:08:28.930
His face was flickering in and out; sometimes
it was human, sometimes it was a mess of eyes

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:30.159
and teeth.

00:08:30.159 --> 00:08:34.890
More demons were sitting at all the slot machines
behind him, pulling levers made from human

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:35.890
bones.

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:38.590
“Good choice, Dolly,” the croupier said.

00:08:38.590 --> 00:08:40.180
“Red suits you.”

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:42.340
He reached out and gave the wheel a spin.

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:47.460
Dolly was lost in an overwhelming, pulsing
cascade of lights, colors, and sounds.

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:52.900
The horrific, tinny noises of slot machines
and the laughter of the demonic croupier.

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:58.940
She was lost in panic and terror as she just
kept spinning, and spinning, and spinning…

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:04.020
When her body was eventually found, her muscles
were in the advanced stages of atrophy, as

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:06.310
though she hadn’t been moving for years.

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:11.550
Her brain was rotted, and the cause of death
was determined as sudden cardiac arrest after

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:15.100
an extended period of sleep-deprivation and
malnutrition.

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:19.540
Dolly’s family would never really know what
happened to her.

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:26.080
The SCP Foundation had learned a lot about
SCP-666 after containing it in Site 73.

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:31.580
Its anomalous effects namely, vivid, complex,
and potentially fatal hallucinatory experiences

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:36.070
only affect those with some kind of addiction
or dependency.

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:40.010
To anyone else entering the Yurt, it’s just
a normal tent.

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:44.770
But any addicts who enter will fall under
its spell, and are generally transported back

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:47.940
to the place they were when their addiction
was at its strongest.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:54.880
There, they’ll encounter SCP-666-1, an entity
that takes the form of a figure from the subject’s

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:59.690
life - normally one heavily involved in facilitating
their addiction.

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:05.850
Any kind of addiction seems to trigger it
- alcohol, drugs, gambling, pornography, food,

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:09.510
self-destructive behavior, even video games.

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:15.620
SCP-666 kills its victims with a twisted mockery
of the very thing they were dependent on in

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:16.620
life.

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:21.050
It was only with Paul Ables that the Foundation
started discovering the other side of the

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:22.050
coin.

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:26.460
By almost all accounts, Paul is a worse person
than anyone on this list.

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:31.130
He got addicted to hard drugs early on in
life, and took up a life of violent crime

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:32.820
to support his habit.

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:37.420
He murdered two people during a liquor store
robbery, before being convicted and sentenced

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:38.420
to death.

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:43.130
He’d then been picked up as a D-Class by
the SCP Foundation, and selected as a test

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:47.700
subject for SCP-666 due to his history of
drug abuse.

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:52.640
The Foundation expected to be adding his body
to the incineration pile sometime soon.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:57.750
None of the researchers working on the case
predicted what actually ended up happening.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:10:59.870
Paul was forced to enter the Yurt.

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:05.020
Not long after, he found himself standing
in a place he’d done everything to try to

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:09.970
forget: The dark, graffiti-stained alley where
he’d been sold his first hit.

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:11.630
This was the place.

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:14.700
The place that destroyed his life.

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:18.500
Every bad decision he’d ever made could
be traced back to here.

00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:21.510
And specifically to one man…

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:22.510
Frank.

00:11:22.510 --> 00:11:28.980
Frank was his first dealer, and how here he
was, standing right across from him once more.

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:30.580
Frank was grinning like a demon.

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:32.270
“C’mon, Paulie,” he said.

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:33.450
“You’re gutter trash.

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:34.463
We both know what you’re going to do.

00:11:34.463 --> 00:11:36.700
Don’t you feel it?

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:38.030
The itch?

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:40.410
One more hit, Paulie, that’s all you need
to feel better.

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:43.010
It’s time to take your medicine…”

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:46.380
Frank - or this thing, pretending to be Frank
- was right.

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:48.070
He did feel the itch.

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:49.530
He did want it.

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:51.090
Maybe he was no better than this.

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:55.530
He began to reach out towards Frank, but then
he abruptly pulled his hand back.

00:11:55.530 --> 00:12:00.490
Frank’s eyes widened in genuine surprise,
as Paul replied, “I’m sorry.

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:03.160
That’s not me anymore.”

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:08.870
SCP-666-1 gave a smile, and said, “Maybe
it isn’t, Paulie, maybe it isn’t…”

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:14.480
When Paul Ables was removed from the SCP-666
Yurt by one of the attending guards, the Foundation

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:17.820
researchers discovered something amazing.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:23.420
Paul was very much alive, and more incredibly,
he didn’t feel the cravings anymore.

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:25.670
His addiction was gone.

00:12:25.670 --> 00:12:30.110
And further tests showed that Paul wasn’t
a one-off event.In fact, anyone who denies

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:37.060
the temptation of SCP-666 will see their addictions
miraculously disappear when they finally leave.

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:42.110
After this it became clear to the Foundation
that SCP-666 isn’t just a way to punish

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:44.970
addicts, it’s a kind of test.

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:48.860
Those who want to change, who are willing
to truly confront their demons and resist

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:54.350
the pull of temptation are rewarded with the
one thing they want most in life, freedom

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:56.510
from their addiction.

00:12:56.510 --> 00:13:00.600
SCP-666 can be your doom or your salvation.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:05.170
And like most things in life, you get what
you give.

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:10.620
Now check out “SCP-610 - The Flesh That
Hates” and “SCP-1730 - What Happened to

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:14.660
Site-13?” for more anomalous places straight
out of your worst nightmares!

